{% comment %}
  A token that changes whenever the site is rebuilt, for appending to the
  stylesheet and script URLs.

  Those two are served as one fixed filename each with a ten-minute freshness
  window, so without this a reader can be handed HTML from the new build and
  keep serving themselves CSS and JS from the old one. Everything that depends
  on the two agreeing -- the bilingual rules and the nav script most visibly --
  is broken for exactly as long as that lasts.

  The build timestamp, not site.github.build_revision. The SHA reads like the
  more honest token -- it changes when and only when the output can have
  changed -- but it comes from jekyll-github-metadata asking the API what the
  repo is at, and that answer can lag the tree the builder actually checked
  out. It did, once: two Pages builds of different content both stamped
  ebcbdaa2, which is the exact collision this file exists to rule out.

  site.time cannot lag, because it is not asking anyone. The cost is that a
  build which changed nothing in these two files still re-fetches them; at
  roughly 200KB, on a site that deploys when something is actually edited, that
  is the cheaper of the two mistakes by a wide margin.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign asset_version = site.time | date: '%s' %}
